@*
    The shell (spec §3.7): one app bar, one nav drawer, the pending bar under the app bar, and the bottom bar under
    720 px.

    The two layouts are one markup tree, not two components. A second component would be a second navigation — and
    §4.3 argues at length that a second navigation is how a navigation starts lying about what works. That is also
    why the drawer's entries are SectionLink rather than the library's nav link (V8): a section here owns routes
    beyond its own prefix (Schema owns /changes, Data the record routes), and the library's link cannot say so.
*@
@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@implements IDisposable
@inject AssistantGateway Assistant
@inject ILogger<AdminLayout> Logger
@inject AdminInterop Interop
@inject IBrowserViewportService Viewport

@*
    The library's four providers, in the interactive layout because the library does not render them statically
    (study §1.3). Exactly one popover provider: a second throws. Dialog defaults are the pattern language's:
    focus lands on the first field, and a click on the scrim never dismisses (spec §3.1-3.2). Each wrapper still
    states its own options.
*@
<AlvoTheme />
<MudPopoverProvider />
<MudDialogProvider DefaultFocus="DefaultFocus.FirstChild" BackdropClick="false" CloseButton="false" />
<MudSnackbarProvider />

<MudLayout Class="a-shell">
    <MudAppBar Dense="true" Elevation="0" Class="a-appbar" data-testid="appbar">
        <AlvoButton Tone="AlvoButton.ButtonTone.Ghost" Small="true" class="a-only-mobile" aria-label="Sections"
                    aria-expanded="@(_navOpen ? "true" : "false")" data-testid="more-sections" OnClick="_ => ToggleNav()">
            <Icon Path="@Icons.More" />
        </AlvoButton>
        <a class="a-brand" href="@AdminPaths.Overview" aria-label="Alvo">
            <img src="@AlvoAdminAssets.Mark" width="26" height="26" alt="" />
            <span>Alvo</span>
        </a>
        <AlvoButton Tone="AlvoButton.ButtonTone.Ghost" Small="true" aria-label="Search" OnClick="_ => OpenPalette()">
            <Icon Path="@Icons.Search" />
            <span>Search</span>
            <kbd class="a-kbd">&#8984;K</kbd>
        </AlvoButton>
        <span class="a-spacer"></span>
        <ThemeToggle />
        <SignedInAs />
    </MudAppBar>

    @*
        Responsive: the persistent drawer on a desktop, a temporary one over the page on a phone, where the
        bottom bar carries the first five sections and the app bar's Sections button opens the rest.
    *@
    <MudDrawer @bind-Open="_navOpen" Variant="DrawerVariant.Responsive" Breakpoint="Breakpoint.Sm"
               ClipMode="DrawerClipMode.Always" Elevation="0" Class="a-navdrawer">
        <nav aria-label="Sections" data-testid="sidebar" class="a-navdrawer__nav">
            <ProjectSwitcher />
            <MudNavMenu Dense="true">
                <NavList OnNavigate="CloseNavOnPhone" />
            </MudNavMenu>
        </nav>
    </MudDrawer>

    <MudMainContent Class="a-main">
        <PendingBar />

        <main class="a-content" id="a-content">
            @*
                A screen that throws while rendering takes this panel instead of the circuit. Without the
                boundary a render bug ends the session: the shell, the working copy's pending count and every
                other screen go with it, and the operator reads the framework's reconnect banner. The
                boundary's own logger writes the exception to the server log, so the panel can say "in the
                server log" and mean it.
            *@
            <ErrorBoundary @ref="_boundary">
                <ChildContent>@Body</ChildContent>
                <ErrorContent Context="fault">
                    <ErrorPanel Fault="@fault">
                        <Actions>
                            <AlvoButton Small="true" data-testid="error-reload" OnClick="_ => Reload()">Reload</AlvoButton>
                        </Actions>
                    </ErrorPanel>
                </ErrorContent>
            </ErrorBoundary>
        </main>

        @*
            Mounted only when a connection resolves. A deployment with no AI gets no launcher, no badge
            and no explanation to read — spec §3.3: a control whose only possible outcome is "not
            configured" is worse than its absence, which is the argument Settings already makes about
            issuing an API key.
        *@
        @if (_assistant)
        {
            <AssistantDrawer />
        }

        @* The pane's last row rather than the layout's, so it sits under the content on a phone and needs no
           height of its own subtracted anywhere. *@
        <nav class="a-bottomnav" aria-label="Sections" data-testid="bottom-nav">
            @foreach (var entry in AdminNavigation.Bar)
            {
                @SectionLink.For(entry, "a-bottomnav__item", "a-bottomnav__item--active", BarContent(entry))
            }
        </nav>
    </MudMainContent>
</MudLayout>

<CommandPalette @ref="_palette" />

@code {
    private CommandPalette? _palette;
    private ErrorBoundary? _boundary;
    private readonly Guid _viewportWatch = Guid.NewGuid();
    private bool _assistant;
    private bool _navOpen = true;
    private bool _phone;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether to mount the assistant, asked at boot and again whenever the connection is saved.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Every refusal is "no launcher", never a failed page.</b> The shell renders for a caller the
    /// project names nowhere and for one whose account has been deleted since they signed in — and both
    /// reach this call. A layout that let either throw would replace the screen that says what happened
    /// with a 500, which is measured rather than imagined: it took out the revoked-session scenario.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Asked again, because once per circuit was a bug rather than a simplification.</b> An operator who
    /// configured the connection on Settings got a panel reporting <c>configured</c> and a shell with no
    /// launcher in it, for the rest of the session and with nothing on screen suggesting a reload. The
    /// subscription is to <see cref="AssistantGateway.ConnectionChanged"/>, which the save raises — see the
    /// argument for the event there.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Assistant.ConnectionChanged += OnConnectionChanged;
        await RefreshAssistantAsync();
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Assistant.ConnectionChanged -= OnConnectionChanged;
        _ = StopWatchingViewportAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Starts following the viewport, then says the shell has rendered interactively, providers and all. It is the
    /// readiness signal a scenario waits on before it opens a popover or a dialog (study §5.1 gotcha 2): a click
    /// before the circuit is live is lost, and so is a phone's first choice in the drawer made before the shell
    /// knows it is on a phone.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            await Viewport.SubscribeAsync(_viewportWatch, OnViewportChanged, fireImmediately: true);
            await Interop.MarkShellReadyAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Below the drawer's breakpoint the drawer is the temporary one, drawn over the page.</summary>
    private Task OnViewportChanged(BrowserViewportEventArgs change) => InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        _phone = change.Breakpoint <= Breakpoint.Xs;
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// Lets the viewport go, unless the circuit already has: a layout disposed with its circuit has no browser left
    /// to stop listening in.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StopWatchingViewportAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await Viewport.UnsubscribeAsync(_viewportWatch);
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException
                                              or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            /* Nothing is left to unsubscribe from. */
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Clears a caught fault on every navigation — the layout's parameters are set again with each new page —
    /// so the panel belongs to the screen that threw rather than to the session.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _boundary?.Recover();

    /// <summary>Draws the screen again from scratch, which re-reads everything it shows.</summary>
    private void Reload() => _boundary?.Recover();

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-asks, then redraws — on the renderer's thread, because the save that raised this ran on the
    /// settings page's own.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnConnectionChanged() =>
        _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            await RefreshAssistantAsync();
            StateHasChanged();
        });

    /// <summary>The one place the question is asked, so the boot and the re-ask cannot answer it differently.</summary>
    private async Task RefreshAssistantAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            _assistant = await Assistant.IsConfiguredAsync(CancellationToken.None);
        }
        catch (Exception exception)
        {
            AdminProblem.Absorb(exception, Logger);
            _assistant = false;
        }
    }

    private Task OpenPalette() => _palette?.OpenAsync() ?? Task.CompletedTask;

    private void ToggleNav() => _navOpen = !_navOpen;

    /// <summary>
    /// A choice in the drawer closes it on a phone, where it covers the page; on a desktop it is the persistent
    /// sidebar and stays. Which one it is follows the viewport, below the drawer's own breakpoint.
    /// </summary>
    private void CloseNavOnPhone()
    {
        if (_phone)
        {
            _navOpen = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A bottom-bar entry: a smaller icon over the section's short title.</summary>
    private static RenderFragment BarContent(AdminSection entry) => @<text>
        <Icon Path="@entry.Icon" Size="18" />
        <span>@entry.ShortTitle</span>
    </text>;
}
